        <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays</span>{r.pays}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>How you earn trust</span>{r.trust}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>{r.watch}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Growth move</span>{r.grow}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The two best-known geography routes, civil services and teaching, are the two we rate Not so
      scalable. That is not an argument against them. It is an argument for not treating them as
      the only plan. Government exams are rank-based, they consume years, and the study rarely
      produces proof the private market can see. Pay in government service follows fixed scales,
      postings and transfers are part of the job, and outside-work rules differ by service, so
      check them for the post you want. Saving and investing can still build wealth, but the job
      itself gives little room to scale. Compare the foregone income of the preparation years, too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> a small GIS or mapping practice. It scales because you
      set the price per project, repeat work comes from the same clients and local bodies, and you
      can later hire a junior or use AI tools for the repeat steps. India's National Geospatial
      Policy 2022 opened the way for private entities to produce and use geospatial data, and a
      published explainer of the policy cited a projection of about 63,000 crore rupees by 2025
      with more than 10 lakh jobs. Treat that as a projection, not a result. Price by the project
      and the outcome, not by the hour, and put scope, price and revisions in writing. Start with one
      niche and one client type, usually after a few years of employed work.
    </p>
  </div>

