  <h2 id="gis-ladder">The GIS ladder: from digitizer to domain analyst</h2>

  <p>
    GIS is the skill that connects most geography routes, so it is the one worth building first. It
    is not one job. It is a ladder, and the rungs pay and age very differently.
  </p>

  <div class="ladder">
    {ladder.map((item) => (
      <div class="ladder-step">
        <div class="ladder-step__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="ladder-step__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    A strong geography skill portfolio has three parts: a core skill (GIS analysis), a multiplier
    skill (SQL or Python, or deep knowledge of one industry such as water or retail), and visible
    proof (a finished project with the method written out). Add clear English writing and you are
    making the decisions that unlock high income opportunities.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">A better adjacent route, for some</p>
    <p>
      If your goal is the widest hiring and the highest ceiling, and you enjoy numbers, a geography
      graduate who adds SQL, statistics and dashboards can aim at data analyst roles, with GIS as
      the edge that others lack. It has more employers than pure mapping. It is a bigger pivot, so
      test it with one project first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Learn it for little or nothing</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Software:</strong> QGIS is free. You can practise on free public satellite images and open map data without paying for licences.</li>
    <li><strong>Courses:</strong> start with IIRS's free online courses, then YouTube tutorials and official software documentation. Pay for a course only if it gives something free material cannot, such as feedback on your project, a recognised credential, or supervised practice.</li>
    <li><strong>Judge each course:</strong> check the instructor's recent work, the last update, whether you build a project, and what past learners did next. A pile of certificates is not a portfolio.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays, and how they find and trust you</h2>

  <p>
    Every career has a "who pays and how do they find you" layer. Most degrees never teach it.
    Here it is for geography, kept to the basics. No route promises an income.
  </p>

